Automated checks belong in CI specifically because they're cheap and catch real regressions before they ship — the floor from earlier, enforced automatically rather than relying on someone remembering to run a scan manually:

```js
// example using @axe-core/playwright in an end-to-end test
import AxeBuilder from '@axe-core/playwright';

test('signup page has no automated a11y violations', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/signup');
  const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
    .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])
    .analyze();

  expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
});
```

Scoping the tags to `wcag2a`/`wcag2aa` matches the AA target from earlier rather than pulling in AAA-only rules that may not be an intentional goal. This test catches regressions — a new component shipped with a missing label, a contrast change that silently drops below 4.5:1 — automatically on every PR, which is exactly the class of bug easiest to introduce accidentally and easiest to catch mechanically, freeing the scarce manual-testing time for the harder judgment calls automation can't make.
